            ]
        }
    ],
    [
        {
            "type": "title",
            "content": {
                "title_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "“Through carve-outs, companies can create value, streamline portfolios, and reduce complexity. But the ultimate success of a deal hinges on focused, proactive execution."
                    }
                ],
                "level": 2
            },
            "bbox": [
                43,
                136,
                587,
                217
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "As the health care environment evolves at an accelerating rate, biopharma companies increasingly respond through carve-outs. By reshaping their portfolios, firms can focus on fast-growing business units and unlock value from assets that add unnecessary complexity or might perform better with a different owner. Yet these transactions won’t achieve their full potential unless companies take the right approach to deal execution. Carve-outs are more challenging in biopharma than in other industries, leading to greater value creation for businesses that execute deals well—and greater risks of destroying value for those that don’t."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                40,
                287,
                932,
                428
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "From our experience helping biopharma leadership teams execute large-scale carve-outs, we’ve learned that companies can proactively manage the complexity of these deals by keeping a laser focus on strategy from the outset and following a few clear supporting principles. In doing so, biopharma players can avoid unexpected one-time costs and ongoing entanglements—getting to the finish line faster. This is a more deliberate and thoughtful approach but yields superior value in the long run."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                40,
                441,
                942,
                561
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "title",
            "content": {
                "title_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Deal Execution Is Where Value Is Gained—or Lost"
                    }
                ],
                "level": 2
            },
            "bbox": [
                43,
                631,
                826,
                708
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Across industries, carve-outs are on the rise. From July 2020 through June 2021, the quarterly average deal value was $309 billion, 25% higher than the five-year average ($247 billion per quarter). There is a clear logic behind this trend: carve-outs create value. From 2003 through mid-2021, returns for the Bloomberg Spin-Off Index more than doubled those of the S&P 500. Yet performance varies widely between the winners and losers. Only about 50% of deals create value in the medium term, and poor performers can lose significant value, producing total shareholder return that’s more than 100 percentage points below the leaders. (See Exhibit 1.)"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                40,
                738,
                934,
                877
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "page_footer",
            "content": {
                "page_footer_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "©2025 Boston Consulting Group"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                43,
                969,
                249,
                984
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "page_number",
            "content": {
                "page_number_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "2"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                942,
                970,
                954,
                982
            ]
        }
    ],
    [
        {
            "type": "title",
            "content": {
                "title_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Exhibit 1 - Carve-Outs Offer Significant Value, but Performance Varies Widely"
                    }
                ],
                "level": 2
            },
            "bbox": [
                48,
                71,
                673,
                107
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "chart",
            "content": {
                "image_source": {
                    "path": "images/9a7d652c4e563f7fd3189b9d36287bd27a91366d398671eb7858f3a996d46ae8.jpg"
                },
                "content": "",
                "chart_caption": [],
                "chart_footnote": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Sources: S&P Capital IQ; Refinitiv Datastream; BCG analysis."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                55,
                126,
                331,
                313
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "chart",
            "content": {
                "image_source": {
                    "path": "images/66ec3f863b37928fd11774575d230ae6e0299a26349753114b92ec71890e3823.jpg"
                },
                "content": "",
                "chart_caption": [],
                "chart_footnote": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Note: The Bloomberg Spin-Off Index tracks US spinoffs for the first three years of their independent operation. For the TSR chart, N = 195 spinoffs completed from 2010 through 2020. TSR = total shareholder return; p.p. = percentage points."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                348,
                127,
                712,
                319
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Often, the differentiating factor is deal execution. No matter how strategically sound a carve-out may seem on paper, if it is not executed in a thoughtful, deliberate way—considering and planning for all contingencies—it will likely fall short of expectations."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                40,
                404,
                947,
                464
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "The carve-out trend in biopharma is just as strong as it is in other industries. Activity in 2020 was artificially low because of the pandemic, but 2019 was an all-time high, with $44 billion in deal value, including four carve-outs of more than $5 billion. (See Exhibit 2.) All signs suggest that divestitures will resume as the business environment slowly returns to normal. Strong valuations, the continued impacts from loss of exclusivity, and pricing pressures are together spurring an increase in carve-outs, as biopharma companies reshape their portfolios to better meet the needs of the market and create value for shareholders."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                40,
                478,
                944,
                618
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "title",
            "content": {
                "title_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Exhibit 2 - Biopharma Divestitures Reached an All-Time High in 2019"
                    }
                ],
                "level": 2
            },
            "bbox": [
                48,
                656,
                671,
                675
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "chart",
            "content": {
                "image_source": {
                    "path": "images/651b153d2c4e39b43622fcac6f2772d9ea1dfb9fd9021429539bdaa4a148d882.jpg"
                },
                "content": "",
                "chart_caption": [],
                "chart_footnote": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Sources: S&P Capital IQ; company reports; BCG analysis."
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Note: Includes both spinoffs and corporate divestitures; deals with unknown values not counted; numbers rounded."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                111,
                695,
                681,
                884
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "page_footer",
